Welcome to STM!  STM (Sarudemo Tsukureru Microprocessor -- never mind
the meaning:-) is a 2-way superscalar, 32-bit RISC CPU.  It is free 
hardware; it is distributed under a condition similar to GPL.

STM comes with ABSOLUTELY NO WARRANTY.  Its specifications MAY BE
CHANGED ANY TIME WITHOUT NOTICE.

This package contains the design data of the CPU, a simulator, an assembler,
and some other tools.  The design has been done with an HDL called 
CHDL, which is my invention and quite close to C language.  It is 
mostly described in RTL (Register Transfer Level).  The CHDL source
code can be converted to VHDL; a converter program is contained in
the package.

Sofar, only logic siml has been done.  I'm planning to implement it
physically with FPGA.  Current (and rough) estimation of the size is
about 30,000 gates, excluding TLB and cache.

Design of the instruction pipeline has been done in RTL.  The Execution
unit and the register file is simulated with a behavioral model.  TLB
and cache have not been designed yet.  It has no FPU.

Though I wouldn't claim that STM is faster than 21164:-), it is 
designed to be *fairly* fast.  Its internal architecture is (hopefully)
comparable to second generation RISCs, such as supersparc and R4000.

The rest of the document is divided into four parts : Architecture,
Hardware Implementation, Tools, and Future Plans.  The Architecture
section describes the programmer's view of STM, and why it was so
designed.  In the next section, the internal architecture and
implementation details are described, especially the pipeline.  The
Tools section is about how the design is simulated, tested, and 
converted to VHDL.  Lastly, I'll tell you about my future plans (dreams?).

